<sect> cmdline (Revision 1.4 -- 05/05/98)

<p>The unit <tt/cmdline/ provides functions to ease the use of
command line parameters in programs based on the Common Library

<sect1>Macros and the Main Option Data Type

<p>The following Macros are defined for use as value to <tt/argtype/
in the part <ref id="mainoptions" name="mainoptions data structure">

<sect2> macro definitions<label id="macro">
<p>
<descrip>
<tag/ CMDL_CHAR/
  The pointer passed to <tt/argument/ will be interpreted as <tt/char */.
  If an argument exists, it is returned as a nullterminated string and
  its space will be allocated by <tt/process_cmdline_opt/. See also section
  <ref id="sample" name="Example of Main Program">.
<tag/ CMDL_INT/
  The pointer passed to <tt/argument/ will be interpreted as <tt/int/.
  It may be initialized in advance to a default value.
<tag/ CMDL_DOUBLE/
  The pointer passed to <tt/argument/ will be interpreted as <tt/double/.
  It may be initialized in advance to a default value.
</descrip>

<sect2> struct mainoptions<label id="mainoptions">
<p>

This structure is used to hold all the valid program options and
additional information used by the two routines described in this chapter.
You must provide either <tt/argument/ or <tt/flag/ or both.

<p>
<tscreen><verb>
struct mainoptions {
	int valid;
	int optchar;
	void *argument;
	int argtype;
	int *flag;
	int valseen;
	char *shortdesc;
	char *longdesc;
}
</verb></tscreen>


<p><tt/int valid/.
A flag for internal checks, you may set this simply to zero.

<p><tt/int optchar/.
The character of the short option, e.g. 'q' if the option is to be <tt/-q/.


<p><tt/void *argument/.
A pointer where to store the agument passed with the option, 
NULL means this option will have no argument.

<p><tt/int argtype/.
The type as which we have to interpret the pointer above.
This must be of of <tt/CMDL_CHAR/, <tt/CMDL_INT/ or <tt/CMDL_DOUBLE/.

<p><tt/int *flag/.
Variable to set to <tt/valseen/, when this option is seen.
Pass NULL if no flag has to be set.
 
<p><tt/int valseen/.
The value to set <tt/flag/ to when option was seen. Do not forget to initialize
<tt/flag/ to a value different from <tt/valseen/ before calling the
processing routine in order see, whether <tt/flag/ has changed.

<p><tt/char *shortdesc/.
Usage information describing the argument in one word:
e.g. passing <tt/&dquot; &lt;input_file&gt;&dquot;/ to an option 'i' will result
in the following help information: <tt/programname [-i &lt;input_file&gt;]/.

<p><tt/char *longdesc/.
Usage information, verbose help line.
This string will be echoed to the used, when he calls the
program with the <tt/-h/ help option:
 e.g. <tt/&dquot; -i   data input file&dquot;/
  
<sect1>Functions to help parse a commandline
<p>

Prior to calling any of these functions, you must initialize a
<tt/mainoptions/ data structure and set the numerical default values.
Refer to section <ref id="sample" name="Example of Main Program">
to see how to do this.

<sect2>print_usage_info
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
void print_usage_info (const int verb, struct mainoptions o[], const int numelem,
                       const char *description, const char *nonopt)
</verb></tscreen>
<p>

This function prints commandline option information from the <tt/mainoptions/
data structure to <tt/stderr/. The variable <tt/numelem/ must contain the
correct number of elements in <tt/mainoptions/. 
If <tt/verb/ is set zero, the <tt/longdesc/ lines will <bf/not/ be printed.

The string passed with <tt/description/ serves as a short information,
what this program does. Using the string <tt/nonopt/, you may provide
usage information about the non option arguments your program will take.

The Information is printed
in the following format:
<tscreen><verb>
programname  ---  description

usage: programname -optchar shortdesc -optchar shortdesc nonopt

longdesc
longdesc
</verb></tscreen>
The program name is taken from the global variable 
<tt/program_invocation_short_name/ defined in <tt/errproc/.


<sect2>process_cmdline_opt
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
int process_cmdline_opt (const int argcount, char **argvect,
                         struct mainoptions o[], const int numelem,
                         const char *description, const char *nonopt);
</verb></tscreen>
<p>
This routine parses the arguments in <tt/argvect/ for the options
in <tt/o/. The variable <tt/numelem/ must contain the
correct number of elements in <tt/o/. The variable <tt/argcount/
must contain the correct number of elements in <tt/argvect/.

The string passed with <tt/description/ serves as a short information,
what this program does. Using the string <tt/nonopt/, you may provide
usage information about the non option arguments your program will take.

<sect3>Return Values<p>
<descrip>
<tag/a positive number/ All values in <tt/argvect/ were successfully parsed.
The return value is the index of the first non-option value in <tt/argvect/.
<tag/-1/ There was an unknown option or no argument to an option which
requires one.
<tag/-2/ The value <tt/argtype/ in the struct <tt/mainoptions/ contains
an invalid value. (e.g. one different from those of
section <ref id="macro" name="macro definitions">).
<tag/-3/ The option <tt/-h/ for help was found and therefore
a help message was printed automagically. Any options
after <tt/-h/ were <bf/not/ looked at.
</descrip>

<sect3>Errors<p>
In the case, one of the error conditions described above occured,
 <tt/process_cmdline_opt/
will print an error message to <tt/stderr/ using the global variable 
<tt/program_invocation_short_name/ defined in <tt/errproc/.


<sect1>Example of Main Program<label id="sample">
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
/*
 *      FILE: sample
 *
 *   PROJECT: GGL Common Applications
 *
 *   PURPOSE: main program sample
 *
 * COPYRIGHT: (c) 1997 Geodesy and Geodynamics Lab,
 *                     ETH Zurich
 *
 *   AUTHORS: Favey Etienne   (c version)
 *
 *   HISTORY: dd-mm-yyyy  --  description
 *            
 *            16-01-1998  --  created
 *
 *   CAVEATS: Needs the GGL Common Library!
 *
 *  Please compile with compiler flags "-lm -lcommon" !
 */

#define SRC_VERSION 1.00
#ifndef LIBVER
#define LIBVER ""
#endif
#define COPYRIGHT "(c) 1998 Geodesy and Geodynamics Lab, ETH Zurich"

#include <errno.h>      /*  the allround headers */
#include <stdlib.h>     /*  the allround headers */
#include <stdio.h>      /*  crucial for file input and output   */
#include <ctype.h>      /*  character handling */
#include <string.h>     /*  string handling */
#include <math.h>       /*  mathematics */

#include <common.h>     /*  our own institute's headers */

/*
 *  Print version information to stderr
 *  You should not need to modify this
 */
void display_version()
{
        extern char *program_invocation_short_name;
        fprintf (stderr,
                 "%s version %3.2f \n  %s\n  %s\n",
                 program_invocation_short_name, SRC_VERSION, LIBVER, COPYRIGHT);
        fprintf (stderr,
                 "  maintainers: E. Favey <favey@geod.ethz.ch>\n\n");
}


/*
 *  That's where we do the work 
 */
int main ( int argc, char **argv)
{
        int ind_read;           /* index of first non option argument */

        /* 
         *  variables to hold arguments from command line parsing
         *  and default settings for command line options 
         */
        int verbose = FALSE;
        int pr_version = FALSE;
        int myflag = FALSE;
        int ivalue = 25;
        double dvalue = 26.4;
        char *aname = NULL;

        /* 
         *  let's define our command line options
         *  -h is already included in library 
         */
        struct mainoptions myopts[] =                   /* from cmdline.h */
        {
                /* a flag type entry */
                {0,'V', NULL, 0, &amp;pr_version, TRUE, "",
                 "Version string."},
                /* a flag type entry */
                {0,'v', NULL, 0, &amp;verbose, TRUE, "", 
                 "Print information while calculating."},
                /* a flag type entry */
                {0,'f', NULL, 0, &amp;myflag, TRUE, "", 
                 "My special flag that will change things."},
                /* an integer argument entry */
                {0,'i', &amp;ivalue, CMDL_INT, NULL, 0, " <ivalue>",
                 "The ivalue you need for xyz."},
                /* an double argument entry */
                {0,'d', &amp;dvalue, CMDL_DOUBLE, NULL, 0, " <dvalue>",
                 "A floating point argument."},
                /* an char argument entry */
                {0,'s', &amp;aname, CMDL_CHAR, NULL, 0, " <your_name>",
                 "Please pass a string :-)"}
        };
        int optcount = (sizeof(myopts)/sizeof(struct mainoptions));

        /*
         *  Description what the program does
         */
        char description[] = "a sample program doing nothing really";

        /*
         *  Information about our non option arguments
         */
        char nonoptarg[] = " <input_filename> [<input_filename> ...]";

        /* 
         *  proper error cursing with our own command name 
         */
        extern char *program_invocation_name;           /* from errproc.h */
        extern char *program_invocation_short_name;     /* from errproc.h */
        
        errinit (argv[0]);                              /* from errproc.h */

        /*
         * Now we process all command line options
         */
        ind_read = process_cmdline_opt (argc, argv, myopts, optcount, 
                                        description, nonoptarg);
        /*
         *  String default values are filled only down here
         */
        if (aname == NULL) aname = strdup("weirdname");

        /*
         *  let's evaluate if the Version flag was set
         */
        if (pr_version) { display_version(); return EXIT_SUCCESS; }

        /*
         *  when we got -3, the user called the -h help command
         */
        if (ind_read == -3) return EXIT_SUCCESS;

        /*
         *  we check if we got an error (ind_read <0) or
         *  if we got not enough option arguments.
         *  ind_read points to the first element of non option arguments
         *  we check that we got at least one of them
         */
        if (ind_read < 0 || argc - ind_read < 1) {
                print_usage_info(FALSE, myopts, optcount, 
                                 description, nonoptarg);
                return EXIT_FAILURE;
        }

        /*
         *    If the verbose flag was chosen, we show the content
         *  of our variables.
         *    At this stage, you could call one of your subroutines
         *  with the information and let it do the rest.
         */
        if (verbose) {
                int i;
                fprintf (stdout, "Myflag : %d\n", myflag);
                fprintf (stdout, "ivalue : %d\n", ivalue);
                fprintf (stdout, "dvalue : %12.8f\n", dvalue);
                fprintf (stdout, "aname  : %s\n", aname);
                fprintf (stdout, "non option arguments :");
                for (i=ind_read; i<argc; i++)
                        fprintf (stdout, " %s", argv[i]);
                fprintf (stdout, "\n");
        }


        /* 
         *  clean exit upon completion of tasks
         */
        return EXIT_SUCCESS;
}
</verb></tscreen>



